        </div>

        <p>
          Two small, narrowly-scoped scripts that fix the same annoyance from different angles: launcher-locked PC
          games that fight you the moment you try to run them from Steam instead of their native storefront.
        </p>

        <div class="project-columns">
          <div>
            <h4>Epic Games → Steam Setup Wizard</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Reads Epic's local manifests to list installed titles and build the correct <code>com.epicgames.launcher://</code> launch URI — the raw exe alone trips entitlement and anti-cheat checks.</li>
              <li>Watches running processes during a real launch to tell a short-lived bootstrap stub apart from the actual long-running game, then configures OSOL automatically.</li>
              <li>Built to fix a concrete case — Hell Let Loose's Epic + EasyAntiCheat launch — with a recipe that generalizes to similar Unreal Engine titles.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4>GTA V Enhanced Steam Launcher</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Adds GTA V Enhanced to Steam as a non-Steam game that launches with a single click — no reaching for a mouse to hit Play in Rockstar Games Launcher.</li>
              <li>Built for couch play: Steam Big Picture, Steam Link, and controller-driven sessions where tabbing out to another launcher is not an option.</li>
              <li>Uses only what Windows and Steam already ship — no third-party runtime dependency to keep patched.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="note">
          <strong>Why it exists:</strong> neither Epic nor Rockstar's launchers were built with Steam's non-Steam-game
          model in mind, and the workarounds require digging through manifest files or process lists by hand. Both
          scripts exist purely because that friction was real, not because either project needed a portfolio entry.
        </div>
      </article>
